<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN"
  "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
  <key>Label</key><string>com.vnx.fleet-pin-audit</string>
  <!--
    absence-is-loud: fleet_pin_audit.py judges every registered project's
    engine pin plus, with a scan root, every pin directory under it, and its
    write-state flag records the verdict as the fleet_pin_audit health
    beacon. A beacon nobody drives dies, and the SessionStart digest then
    reads the component as absent. This job is the driver, nothing else: it
    runs the measurement on a cadence and writes one beacon. The audit itself
    is read-only.

    Full scope is mandatory: write-state is refused (exit 2, no beacon) when
    no scan root is given, because a beacon over the registry alone would
    report a partial fleet as healthy. The scan root below is the operator's
    development directory; adjust it to the tree that holds this machine's
    pins when installing. $HOME is expanded by the shell, never baked in.

    Interval: every 6h (21600s), the same reasoning as
    com.vnx.fleet-role-drift. The tool writes its beacon with
    expected_interval_seconds=86400, and health_beacon.all_beacons() reads a
    beacon older than that as stale. A StartInterval at or above 86400 would
    make the beacon stale by construction. 21600s gives 4 runs a day: even 3
    consecutive missed runs (an 18h gap) still land inside the window with
    margin. RunAtLoad makes the first run happen on install, so the beacon
    exists right away.

    A non-zero exit is expected and normal, not a job failure. Exit 1 means
    at least one finding (a dead or unregistered pin, a mislabeled or
    writable release dir, or a pin more than the configured number of
    releases behind). The beacon carries the same verdict as status fail.
    Exit 2 means the meter could not measure; the beacon records it as
    unmeasured, which every reader judges fail, never ok. Do not wire this
    job's exit code into anything that fails a build.

    The store is NOT pinned by path. The job identifies its project through
    VNX_PROJECT_ID (below); the ambient resolver derives the data and state
    directories from that id, and the beacon lands in that project's health
    directory. A consumer installing this template for their own project MUST
    set VNX_PROJECT_ID to their project id; reload_plist.sh substitutes the
    placeholder from its second argument, then from VNX_PROJECT_ID, then from
    the .vnx-project-id marker.

    vnx init is not wired to install this job. Install it manually:
      bash scripts/launchd/reload_plist.sh com.vnx.fleet-pin-audit
  -->
  <key>ProgramArguments</key>
  <array>
    <string>/bin/bash</string>
    <string>-c</string>
    <string>cd ${VNX_HOME} &amp;&amp; exec python3 scripts/fleet_pin_audit.py --write-state --scan-root $HOME/Development --json</string>
  </array>
  <key>StartInterval</key>
  <integer>21600</integer>
  <key>RunAtLoad</key>
  <true/>
  <key>StandardOutPath</key><string>/tmp/vnx-fleet-pin-audit.log</string>
  <key>StandardErrorPath</key><string>/tmp/vnx-fleet-pin-audit.err</string>
  <key>EnvironmentVariables</key>
  <dict>
    <key>VNX_HOME</key>
    <string>${VNX_HOME}</string>
    <key>VNX_PROJECT_ID</key>
    <string>${VNX_PROJECT_ID}</string>
    <key>PATH</key>
    <string>/opt/homebrew/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin</string>
  </dict>
</dict>
</plist>
